A look at Mozilla's Rust
========================

So, Mozilla released version 0.1 of its upcoming programming language 
[Rust](http://www.rust-lang.org/). I read the tutorial that presents 
most of its aspects, and I found a few things worth noting, in terms of 
programming language design.

Types
-----

Rust supports natively all primitive C types: integers, signed and 
unsigned from 8 to 64 bits, floats and doubles, booleans, and a nil 
type, which has a single value. There's also pointer sized integers 
`int` and `uint`.

There's also a `char`, which is a 32 bit Unicode codepoint, and a native 
string `str`, which is basically a vector of bytes, UTF-8 encoded. They 
argue that in most cases it is sufficient and more efficient than a 
vector of characters, and conversion between the too is built-in.

Then there are composites: the vector, noted `[T]` (hey, that was my 
syntax too for sool!), a tuple `(T1,T2)` (sool kind of supports that in 
multiple return values and multiple expressions), and the most important 
one, the record `{field1: T1, field2: T2}`.

The record is a value type that serves like a C struct. The most 
important thing to remember is that it is immutable by default. The 
normal way to "modify" a record is to create a new one using the old one 
as basis:

	let oldpoint = {x: 10f, y: 20f};
	let newpoint = {x: 0f with oldpoint};

That could make an interesting shortcut in sool, where structs are 
mutable:

	oldpoint = Point(20,30)
	-- this:
	other = oldpoint with x = 25 end
	-- is the same as:
	other = oldpoint
	other.x = 25

For objects it could be an implicit copy, and modify some members. The 
syntax `with` exists already, it's the object/struct member initializer:

	point = Point with
		x = 20, y = 30
	end

Well, it's interesting, but not enough to make a feature.

The power of Rust's type system also comes from enums, that are 
basically a way to group different types together, and feels very 
much like functional programming (I think of Caml for instance).

	enum shape {
		circle(point, float),
		rectangle(point, point)
	}

Combined with some powerful pattern system, you get fancy stuff like:

	fn area(sh: shape) -> float {
		alt sh {
			circle(_, size) { float::consts::pi * size * size }
			rectangle({x, y}, {x: x2, y: y2}) { (x2 - x) * (y2 - y) }
		}
	}

That is definitely something we'd do in Caml. You can also define 
"optional" or "nullable" types like this, using generic types (their 
equivalent of patterns):

	enum option<T> { some(T), none }

That goes well with their concept that there can be no NULL pointer. 
Instead, use an option type, and pick the `none`. That's a common 
pattern, it exists in Java as well. I had thought of something like that 
when discussing `nil`: have every type support an optional variant, on 
which we do nil checks, and a non-nullable one, which assumes the 
pointer is always valid. But that brought lots of complications and I 
chose to drop it.

Pointers
--------

Since all records are value types, they are entirely contained in the 
variables. In other words, they are always on the stack, or in 
registers, and copied around in assignments and function calls. For big 
records, that could be inefficient, so there is the possibility of 
"boxing" them into heap allocated objects.

The first kind is the "shared box", which happens to be 
reference-counted, with support for reference cycles, so that's 
equivalent to garbage collection. That's just like any object in sool.

	let x = @10;
	let y = x;

We box the integer 10, and refer to it with both `x` and `y`. When both 
variables go out of scope, the box is freed.

The second kind is the "unique box", which complies to the unique owner 
concept. Rings a bell? In the beginnings of sool, I had thought of 
solving memory management by forcing an object to be referred to by 
a single variable at any time. Then "ownership" of the object could be 
passed around between variables, which would be unset afterwards.

	let x = ~10;
	let y <- x;

Box the integer 10 into a single box, and then pass it to variable 
`y`. `x` just doesn't exist anymore after that statement. That's 
even more powerful than the mechanism I had proposed for sool, which 
was impractical to apply systematically. Indeed, in my system, the 
ownership transfer `y <- x` simply set `x` to nil. In Rust, there 
are no NULL pointers. The compiler ensures that pointers are always 
initialized. All the mechanisms that use nil in Lua or sool are 
replaced by the complex type system in Rust, in particular the 
`option<T>` type. If no pointers are ever NULL, there is certainly 
no nil-check ever, which sounds like a pretty good deal. I'd have to 
dig deeper to see how well that works, but it sounds fun.

The reason for these two different boxings in Rust is that apparently, 
it's meant to be a very concurrent language. Each thread (or "task" as 
they call it) has its own heap, and shared boxed cannot leave a task. 
Unique boxes can safely be passed to another task, since the giver 
forgets about it.

And if I read correctly, there has been a lot of reflexion to design 
the whole language around these tasks. For instance that's why 
records are immutable by default. It means they can be efficiently 
shared by reference between tasks, even though they are value types.

Functions
---------

Rust has regular first-level functions, has one would expect, but also 
fully supports closures. And to comply with the spirit of memory 
protection and tasks of the language, there are also three kinds of 
closures:

- stack closures, that can access their full environment but cannot be 
stored: they are used right here and now
- boxed closures, that are equivalent to Lua's: they take a copy of 
their environment on the heap, and you can store them anywhere
- unique closures: like boxed, but they also own every value in the 
copied environment, so they can be safely passed to another task

Object oriented patterns
------------------------

So far, we've seen that Rust is an imperative language, with some 
elements of functional programming (enum types, patterns, functions take 
values, return values, etc.) But it is not fundamentally an 
object-oriented language.

However, you can define what they call interfaces, that simply list 
function prototypes, and then you can implement these interfaces for 
given types. It looks like Objective-C's protocols, or C++'s purely 
virtual classes, but more from the outside: you add support for a 
certain set of method to a certain type.

Within the implementation, you can use `self`, and to call these 
functions on object, the `obj.method(args)` syntax, as expected.

This combined with some kind of pattern matching (when defining 
templates you can request that the type complies to a certain 
interface), and the enum types, and a bit of "casting to interface", is 
a replacement for pure OOP.

That's a very different paradigm, and I suppose it's just as powerful. 
Very odd to look at the first time though.

Foreign function interface
--------------------------

Unsurprisingly, Rust supports FFI. After all, difficult to make a web 
browser without being able to access system calls or GUI libraries. 
Since Rust supports all C types natively, and that the Rust record is 
guarantied to have the same memory layout as C structs, the thing is 
almost trivial. You define a module with C function prototypes, and 
then you can call them directly.

Modulo some details like calling convention, pointer safety and stuff 
like that, this is quite straightforward, like in sool.

Arrays and string
-----------------

One last thing I forgot to mention. At the moment, Rust arrays 
("vectors") are immutable by default, and internized. Even though they 
are on the heap and dynamic, they act as value types.

And strings are both internized and immutable, the Lua way, and soon the 
sool way. That's probably the way to go.

Syntax
------

It's very C like, with some modifications. The biggest one is probably 
that types are written after the name they qualify, and keywords are 
usually very short (`fn` for function kind of shocks me a bit).

Control structure are also expressions, in that each branch can "return" 
a value:

	let a = if true {
		5
	} else {
		10
	};

That can be useful in long switch statement. And again, that helps the 
"no uninitialized variable" concept. In Lua, the rare time when I need 
to declare a local without initializing it is precisely this:

	local a
	if something then
		a = 20
	else
		a = 30
	end

Good luck, Rust!
----------------

That and a gazillion more things make Rust a fairly complex and exotic 
looking language. It's still in development, but it already shows some 
interesting concepts. It's very far from what I envisioned for sool, 
obviously, but it's always fun to see that some concepts are very 
similar to things I thought about during the designing of sool.

Funny detail: the Rust compiler is implemented in Rust itself. To 
bootstrap it, you need a pre-compiled compiler, and they don't explain 
where it comes from. Magic! The first compilers were apparently written 
in OCaml (hey, didn't I say that these enum types + patterns looked like 
Caml?)

Also, it uses LLVM as a backend. Great minds think alike!

I hope they succeed in their endeavor. Despite what some might say (see 
comments on Slashdot), creating new languages is always a good idea. The 
world of computer programming changes, and the way to see programs as 
well. C++ and Java are still the big winners in terms of popularity, and 
even the tremendous rise of Lua will not dislodge them (they don't 
have the same purpose anyway). But maybe a new language paradigm will 
appear, and then we'll be looking back and think "man what were we 
thinking with OOP".

Google also has its own language, Go, which I might also review at some 
point, and it is also not a pure OO language. It has a similar concept 
of separate interfaces and specific implementation of these interfaces 
for the types you want.

D, that I like quite a lot, is on the other hand clearly 
object-oriented, with explicit classes and inheritence. It was meant as 
"a better C++", or "what C++ should have been". Different direction, 
different result, but with some common goals (memory safety, no 
uninitialized variables, boundary checks on arrays, etc.)

sool is definitely not meant to challenge big general purpose languages 
like these. It's a nice little toy that fits my personal programming 
style. I'm not trying to revolutionize anything, just gather the things 
that I believe make sense in a single language. Who knows, it might 
work. It might get some attention. And if it doesn't, fine by me!

I wonder what the future of languages will be.
